WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:04.710
Here's a friendly piece of advice for anyone
who sees Dave Chappelle out and about: don't

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.319
mention Rick James.

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:09.940
There's a decent chance he'll remind you what
the five fingers said to the face.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.100
"What did the five fingers say to the face?"

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:14.960
"What?"

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:15.740
"Slap!"

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.180
Although his short-lived Comedy Central series,
Chappelle's Show, became an instant hit and

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:26.521
one of the best-selling TV shows released
on DVD, there's plenty about the show that

00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:29.020
even the most dedicated fans may not know.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.580
Here's the untold truth of Chappelle's Show.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:38.180
(Chappelle's Show theme playing)

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.500
Before the show

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.250
As a budding actor and stand-up comedian,
Dave Chappelle faced plenty of setbacks, losing

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.550
three times on Star Search and getting booed
off the stage at the Apollo.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:55.690
"I just remember looking out and seeing like
everybody booing, everybody!"

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.380
And his early TV efforts also flopped.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:03.410
The first, a 1996 spin-off from Home Improvement
called Buddies, hit turbulence right out of

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.620
the gate with recasting and poor ratings.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:08.290
It was canceled after only a few episodes
had aired.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:14.470
The next, a 1998 show simply called Dave Chappelle,
didn't even get that far: after Fox executives

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:18.300
wanted to add white actors to the all-black
cast, Chappelle walked away.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.620
"Expect this, my resignation, effective immediately.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.940
I quit!"

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:23.940
Foreshadowing?

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:30.770
"This really is The Wayne Brady Show, no longer
The Chappelle Show, Dave has been downsized."

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:34.479
Just as his Hollywood career began to gain
traction with roles in movies like The Nutty

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:39.010
Professor and Con Air, Chappelle met teenage
funnyman Neal Brennan.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:42.890
Brennan and Chappelle collaborated on the
script for the stoner cult classic Half Baked,

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.880
but after it tanked at the box office, they
parted ways.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:50.680
"Me and Neal, after Half Baked, went our separate
ways.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.760
We were good friends, but it was just like
leaving a crime scene or something."

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.439
How the show was born

00:01:56.439 --> 00:02:02.320
Chappelle's Show had an unlikely inspiration:
Hugh Hefner's casual 1960s talk show Playboy

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.320
After Dark.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.600
"Ya know we did the show, when we were first
coming up with the format we're like 'Man,

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.419
why hasn't anyone ever done a show like this
before?'"

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:13.420
He re-teamed with Neal Brennan to develop
the new show idea.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:17.239
Neither had much experience with sketch comedy,
but they learned how to write sketches by

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:19.239
reading books about Saturday Night Live.

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:23.310
Soon, Chappelle and Brennan pitched the show
to networks, with mixed results.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.189
"Like we took that pitch to HBO and Comedy
Central.

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:29.129
HBO looked at us like we were lepers.

00:02:29.129 --> 00:02:35.859
And then we went to Comedy Central, they approved,
and so we made the pilot."

00:02:35.859 --> 00:02:40.219
One of the two sketch ideas that sold Comedy
Central on the series was "Popcopy," which

00:02:40.219 --> 00:02:41.959
later turned up on Chappelle's Show.

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:45.790
"Be sure to spray chocolate sauce on the wall
near the toilet to give the appearance of

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.319
errant feces.

00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:51.400
This will ensure that any customer who uses
our restroom will never ever return to one

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.969
of our stores."

00:02:52.969 --> 00:02:54.559
Clayton Bigsby

00:02:54.559 --> 00:02:59.209
The pilot episode of Chappelle's Show included
what has become one of the most famous sketches

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:04.739
in sketch comedy history, the story of Clayton
Bigsby, a blind white supremacist who doesn't

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:06.659
know that he himself is black.

00:03:06.659 --> 00:03:12.599
"So we figured we'd make it easier on Clayton
by just telling him and all the other blind

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:15.699
kids that he was white."

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.120
"And he never questioned it?"

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:19.520
"Why would he?"

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.240
Perhaps the craziest part?

00:03:21.249 --> 00:03:26.180
Clayton Bigsby was actually based on a real
person—Dave's own grandfather, who was not

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.560
only of mixed racial heritage, but also blind.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.999
Brennan told Vulture that "The day Martin
Luther King got shot, apparently [Chappelle’s

00:03:33.999 --> 00:03:37.579
grandfather] was on a bus and a bunch of black
dudes came up to him and were like, 'What

00:03:37.579 --> 00:03:41.680
you doing on this bus, cracker?' and Dave's
grandfather apparently thought, Man, this

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.279
cracker is in a lot of trouble, before he
realized, Oh, I'm the cracker."

00:03:46.279 --> 00:03:47.919
Truth is stranger than fiction.

00:03:47.919 --> 00:03:52.609
"We can meet, talk about white brotherhood.

00:03:52.609 --> 00:03:56.629
Thank y'all for comin', white power!"

00:03:56.629 --> 00:03:58.389
He's Rick James...sort of

00:03:58.389 --> 00:04:03.709
Chappelle's impersonation of R&amp;B legend Rick
James became instantly iconic.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:10.020
"I'm Rick James -----. Enjoy yourself."

00:04:10.029 --> 00:04:14.209
But he began hearing the character's catchphrases
thrown back at him — often during the worst

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:15.930
possible moments.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:20.750
It happened during an infamous stand-up performance
just weeks after the season 2 finale aired,

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:24.230
prompting Chappelle to blast the crowd for
their "stupid" behavior.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.990
He considered his stand-up to be the central
element of his career and told the audience,

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.780
"This show is ruining my life."

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.160
It's almost enough to wish he were someone
else.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.060
"Charlie Murphy!"

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.040
"That was cold blooded!"

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.910
Jumping ship

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:47.810
Comedy Central offered Chappelle a whopping
$50 million to make seasons 3 and 4 of Chappelle's

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:48.310
Show.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:49.900
"I'm rich biotch!"

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:54.240
He accepted, but it wasn't long before he
hit a wall with a sketch about magic pixies

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:56.850
who encourage people to live up to racial
stereotypes.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.030
"I'll have the fried chicken, thank you."

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:00.360
"Fried chicken!

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:01.700
I need some music for this!"

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:10.310
The reaction he got from a white crew member
made him reconsider what he was doing.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:14.830
"I know the difference between people laughing
with me and people laughing at me.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:19.720
And it was the first time I'd ever gotten
a laugh, that I was uncomfortable with."

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:23.900
Comedy Central eventually aired the sketch
anyway, against Chappelle’s wishes, along

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:27.290
with a disclaimer and subsequent audience
discussion about the sketch's merits.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:32.010
"As you may or may not have heard, Dave said
he had some problems with the pixie sketch."

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:36.770
But by that point, Chappelle had infamously
quit the show and hopped a plane to Africa.

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:41.360
Rumors swirled that he was having a mental
breakdown, or was on drugs, or abducted by

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.360
aliens.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:43.360
The truth?

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.380
He just needed to get away from something
that was no longer under his control.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:53.090
"Don't like having to beg for the spotlight
man, ya know the machine is good for us and

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:57.770
we good for the machine and it should be fair
man."

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:58.770
Aftermath

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:03.650
Chappelle and Brennan's friendship was severely
damaged after the show's demise, and it took

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:06.140
years to repair what was broken between them.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:10.280
But the two reconnected years later when Brennan
dropped by one of Chappelle's intimate stand-up

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:11.450
shows.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:15.560
Although they've agreed to never work together
again, they remain friends.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:16.560
Brennan told Buzzfeed:

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.580
"I think there's part of both us that holds
each other very personally responsible for

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:21.910
our part of it.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:25.630
But another part of us that sees the whole
thing as very circumstantial."

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.140
More "lost" Sketches

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:31.910
While Comedy Central compiled most of the
completed sketches for season three into "The

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:36.910
Lost Episodes," which aired in the summer
of 2006, other unaired sketches have occasionally

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:40.430
popped up on DVD extras or online bootlegs.

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:45.630
Among them are sketches like the "Super Size
Me" parody "Maximize Me," the recurring "Hip-Hop

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:50.400
Newsbreak" segment, and an R-rated sequel
to Daddy Day Care starring Charlie Murphy.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:51.460
"You asked for it!"

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:52.460
"Woah!"

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:54.760
"Hahahaha!"

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:59.380
And music fans can also track down rare performances
by John Legend, Dead Prez, and The Beastie

00:06:59.389 --> 00:07:00.850
Boys among others.

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:05.030
"And let all the party people feel the beat..."

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:08.120
"...drop!"

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.360
So...who's that guy doing the Robot?

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.020
His name is Karl Lake, and he was a set dresser
for the show.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:18.740
In a first season episode, Chappelle asked
him to appear in a crowd scene and do something

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:19.740
funny.

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:20.740
Naturally, Lake did the Robot.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:24.900
It was so well received that for the second
season, writers made sure to find a place

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.639
for Lake to do a repeat performance at some
point in every episode.

00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:32.770
One thing’s for certain: if Dave Chappelle
ever brings his show back, we hope he plans

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:34.190
to bring Lake with him...

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:37.460
"Hey help us robot, help us!

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:38.461
Robot, help us!"

00:07:38.461 --> 00:07:39.680
Thanks for watching!

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:43.500
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.330
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Chappelle's Show sketch was your favorite...

